<ArticleLayout
  title="5 Signs Your Roof Needs Repair Before Winter"
  description="Catching roof damage early is the cheapest repair there is. Here are the five warning signs our inspectors check first on Boulder homes."
  datePublished="2026-01-15"
  author="Summit Roofing Co."
  image={blogImage}
  imageAlt="Close-up of asphalt shingles showing hail damage and granule loss"
>
  <p>
    Colorado winters are hard on roofs. A small problem in October can become a ceiling stain —
    or worse — by February. These are the five warning signs our inspectors check first, and any
    one of them is worth a closer look before the snow arrives.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">1. Missing or curling shingles</h2>
  <p>
    Wind lifts shingle edges, and once the seal breaks the shingle starts to curl. Curled shingles
    shed water poorly and are the first thing to go in the next storm.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">2. Granules in your gutters</h2>
  <p>
    Asphalt shingles lose granules as they age. A handful after a storm is normal; gutters full of
    grit after every rain means the shingle surface is breaking down.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">3. Damaged flashing</h2>
  <p>
    The metal around chimneys, vents, and valleys fails before the shingles do. Cracked caulk and
    lifted flashing cause most of the leaks we repair.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">4. Daylight in the attic</h2>
  <p>
    On a sunny day, turn off the attic lights and look up. Any pinpoints of daylight are holes
    that water will find long before you do.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">5. Sagging along the ridge</h2>
  <p>
    A sagging roofline suggests moisture has reached the decking. This one can't wait — schedule
    an inspection immediately.
  </p>

